WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.678
- [Kelly Clarkson] Have a
seat. You look gorgeous.

00:00:01.678 --> 00:00:03.477
- Thank you. So do you.

00:00:03.477 --> 00:00:04.530
- Oh, it's nice to see you.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:07.733
We were just both like a
month ago at the Emmys.

00:00:07.733 --> 00:00:10.240
- I know. I was so happy to see you there.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.060
I was like, there's my girl up there.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.080
- Did you have fun at it?

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.430
'Cause I wasn't sitting at it.
- I had so much fun.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:15.263
- I had to come in and out.

00:00:15.263 --> 00:00:16.200
I was doing another job

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.191
so I came in and out-

00:00:17.191 --> 00:00:18.024
- Of course you were.

00:00:18.024 --> 00:00:19.230
- I was jealous that y'all got to eat.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.220
I was like, wait, what?

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.050
- Well, that was a first.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.670
The Emmys this year was really different

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.530
because in the past it's always just been

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.080
you sit in the audience
and you watch the show.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.730
- [Kelly Clarkson] Oh, I'd never been.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.770
- Yeah, you sit in the
audience, you watch the show

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.750
and there's no food,
there's not even any water.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.863
And by the end you're like,
(groans) glamor is so hard.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.640
So this year they put
tables around the stage.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.680
There were tables all over
the place and there was wine

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:44.513
on the tables.

00:00:44.513 --> 00:00:46.290
We were like, yeah, it's a party now.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.800
But it was pretty funny because our table,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.150
we were like front and
center, but behind the stage.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.710
- [Kelly Clarkson] Oh, you
were behind the walk thing.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.870
- Were behind the like, walkway stage.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.490
And so everything that was happening

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:03.600
on the stage was pointed away from us.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.436
So we had amazing views
of everybody's butts.

00:01:08.436 --> 00:01:09.450
(audience laughs)

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:10.440
I mean, I'll tell you-
- You're welcome.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.030
- Anything you wanna know
about anybody's butts.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.610
So I got to see you present.
- You're welcome.

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.470
- I got to see your cute,
cute, cute, cute butt.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.270
And it was really one of
the best of the nights.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:20.103
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:20.103 --> 00:01:22.410
So I wanna say congratulations to you.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.150
- I wanted to say like, it was so cool,

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.110
I'd never been to the Emmys.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.520
So I walked out and I was like, wait,

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:27.990
they get like food and drink at this one?

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:28.823
I was like-

00:01:28.823 --> 00:01:30.750
- It was totally new.
- Why is this my first time?

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.250
- It's never happened before.
- I didn't know.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.083
It was so fun.

00:01:33.083 --> 00:01:34.140
Did you get to meet anyone

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.370
or talk to anyone that you really were-

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.140
- Oh my gosh, I had so much fun.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.340
Yeah, you know, that's just
such an incredible experience

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.140
'cause you see all the
people who have been

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.210
your inspiration your whole life.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:49.320
But that night I got
to meet Henry Winkler.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.640
- I love him.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.340
- I was doing an interview
and it was right next to him,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.140
and I was supposed to be behaving myself

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:56.790
and paying attention and
talking to the person.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:57.623
But I couldn't help it.

00:01:57.623 --> 00:01:59.265
I was like, ah!

00:01:59.265 --> 00:02:02.460
And I just started freaking
out over Henry Winkler because,

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.820
and luckily he is the
kindest man on the planet.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.390
But I said to him, I said,
you know, he was The Fonz.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:10.223
- [Kelly Clarkson] I get it.

00:02:10.223 --> 00:02:13.890
- The Fonz! Fonzie. "Happy Days."

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:14.940
Those were the shows,

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.680
I grew up at that time when there were all

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:17.910
these great sitcoms on,

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.560
those were the shows
that really inspired me

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.720
and made me wanna be an actress.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.270
And he was, you know,
the coolest of the cool.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.317
And the thing that I told him, I said,

00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:29.040
"You inspired me so
much at such a young age

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.690
to want to do this,

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:34.290
but now you continue to
inspire me because you are

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:39.290
so graceful and so humble and
so kind and so enthusiastic."

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:40.800
- Somebody to model ourselves after.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.630
- He's been doing this
his whole entire life.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.130
Years and years and years and years.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.713
And it seems every time you see him speak,

00:02:47.911 --> 00:02:49.770
he is just as thrilled that
he woke up that morning

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:53.490
and got to go to work as he
probably was 50 years ago.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:54.660
And I just think he's great.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:55.770
- He is, and I love that you're doing that

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.240
'cause we always hear
bad stuff about people.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.550
But he is legit the
nicest on and off camera.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.070
And I actually first met him
when I was like 19 years old

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.110
here in LA before anything.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.570
And I did something and
he was directing it and

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.430
he was so nice to like,
we were extras like,

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:09.570
you know what I'm saying?

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:10.740
Like he didn't have to be.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:12.450
And he was so nice to everyone and like,

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:13.530
treated everyone equally.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.390
And it was like, really cool to see that.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.670
- I mean that's just, you know,

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.650
you want that in this business.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.720
You want it in the world, period.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.970
And people feel that
and that's just really-

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.320
- [Kelly Clarkson] And that's
why he's still killing it.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:26.153
- Yeah.

00:03:26.153 --> 00:03:28.110
- So you're co-star from
"White Lotus," Sydney Sweeney,

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.630
she was here and she was fanning over you,

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.150
just saying how incredible that you were.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.520
And you've become like one
of her biggest fans, right?

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.550
- Well, she is, I just
find her so impressive.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:40.500
You know, we met for the first time when

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:41.333
we were doing "White Lotus."

00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:43.050
She was playing my daughter.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.720
I can't believe it that
now I have a series

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.260
of these incredible young women
who have been my daughters.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.810
I feel like now I have
all these TV daughters.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.690
But Sydney, there's something about her.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.590
She's so incredibly talented and

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.270
becoming so incredibly successful.

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:04.650
I mean she's just really
like, honed into who she is.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:08.190
And I really admire that
at such a young age to

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.830
be able to tap into that but
also stay really grounded.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:12.300
She's super close with her family.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:13.320
She, you know-

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:14.580
- It's hard to do in this industry.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:15.573
- It really is.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.630
So yeah. I really admire her.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.700
- I love this about this story.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.190
I think my favorite part
is just the supporting

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:24.023
of other women.

00:04:24.023 --> 00:04:24.960
Like, how important do you think that is?

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.270
'Cause I feel like we always
get pitted against each other.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:28.770
And it's not fair

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:32.370
to other women and to society
in general, but also to us,

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.440
'cause it's like, no
one's thinking that way.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.760
- Well, you know, it's funny that you,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.190
I mean, that to me has been
one of the most important parts

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:41.580
of my career really,
just really of my life.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:42.510
Because no matter what I,

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:45.990
if I was, you know, a
teacher in a public school,

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.300
I would still be wanting
to do the exact same thing,

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.610
and that is to support my fellow humans,

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:52.920
but particularly my fellow women and like,

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.150
lift each other up.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.400
And you know, it's funny
talking about the Emmys

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.107
the other night because in my
category there were five of us

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.590
from my show, the "White Lotus,"

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:06.960
that were nominated in my category.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.460
- I mean, congratulations being a part

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:09.930
of such a killer cast.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:10.763
That's what I'm saying. That's so amazing-

00:05:10.763 --> 00:05:12.180
- Well no, it's an
amazing cast. Thank you.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.560
- That's an honor that you're

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.720
with all these people
that are, that's cool.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:16.623
- It was so cool.

00:05:16.623 --> 00:05:18.660
I've never had an experience like that.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.674
And everybody was like, "Oh, you know,

00:05:20.674 --> 00:05:22.560
are you feeling competitive?"

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:24.630
And I thought, oh my gosh.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:29.070
I could not feel more thrilled
that I get to be there

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:30.780
with my fellow actresses.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.320
Like, look at you being lifted up,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.750
like my fellow actresses in the category.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.070
- And a select few.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:39.960
- We can like do that for each other

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:40.950
and acknowledge each other.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:43.680
And by the way, I'm inspired
by each and every one of them.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.053
So it's just to me like, boy,

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.230
if we can hold each other
and support each other

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:52.470
in a world that wants to tear
us down, that's the goal.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:53.760
- But also, maybe that's why you all

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:54.660
were nominated together,

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:55.950
because when you do have a cast that

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.780
is supportive like that
and you all actually

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:59.070
do wanna lift each other up,

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:01.260
the project becomes even better.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.610
The chemistry becomes magical.

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:03.443
- I think so.

00:06:03.443 --> 00:06:05.250
And you know, that show in particular,

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:06.330
we shot it during COVID.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:08.370
It was like really like the beginning of

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:09.870
the scary part of COVID.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:10.860
We were in a real bubble.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.870
So we really, we had that camaraderie,

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:14.340
and I think that was really special.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.225
- We are back with Connie Britton, y'all.

00:06:16.225 --> 00:06:17.430
(audience clapping)

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:19.507
That was a look at her new Netflix movie,

00:06:19.507 --> 00:06:21.330
"Luckiest Girl Alive."

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:24.540
So can you tell us about
the movie and who you play?

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:27.090
- Well, "Luckiest Girl
Alive," you know, I'm not,

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.221
I can't, (chuckles) I
can't tell you too much.

00:06:30.221 --> 00:06:31.054
(Kelly laughs)

00:06:31.054 --> 00:06:32.970
I've been sworn to secrecy, so.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:34.980
But you know there is,

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:38.110
there's a lot in the movie that happens

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.700
that deals with some pretty
heavy subject matter.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.080
- Yeah.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:46.230
- And, but I play,

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:48.870
again, I'm in sort of a
mother-daughter relationship

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:50.580
with Mila Kunis's character.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.083
And you know, it felt
really important to me.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:07:00.720
I love storytelling as a
way to help people grow

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.250
and learn in the culture.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:03.810
- Perspective.
- And you know,

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:05.610
the movie deals with some things

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:06.900
that are really pretty traumatic

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.610
and culturally important for us to look at

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:14.310
and understand why these
things are happening

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.080
in our culture, you know,

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.840
various forms of violence in our culture.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.240
And so I really wanted to do it

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.840
because I play Mila Kunis's mom.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.323
And in doing that,

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:33.720
it helps when I show that,
when we show that relationship

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.810
it helps remind us that we all have trauma

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:38.550
and we pass it down from generation

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:40.590
to generation to generation.

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:43.170
And when we keep just
passing down the trauma

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:46.740
instead of stopping to understand

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.020
that we need to change
that trauma and we need

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.720
to change the cycle of violence,

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:53.430
then nothing's gonna change.

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:57.570
And so, I really hope that the
movie is gonna be impactful

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:01.237
for people in terms of
just having a glimpse at,

00:08:01.237 --> 00:08:04.320
"Oh, if I can stop the cycle

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.320
of hurt in my own family
or in my own community,

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.230
that can make a difference going forward."

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:14.820
- Yeah, you just reminded me of something

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:16.670
that Jesse actually told me one time.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.410
I don't know know where she
got it, a friend or something.

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.900
But it was, if you change,

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:23.550
'cause I was having a hard
time and it's very hard,

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:25.740
generational trauma or trauma
that you're going through.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.510
But if you could change one thing,

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:29.940
if you could just change
one thing that you're doing

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:32.340
that you feel like is
negative or not helpful,

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.530
then the whole circle widens and changes.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:35.820
And I think that that's so important,

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:38.160
like what you're talking
about in the show.

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:41.430
So I think these stories
truly make a big impact

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:43.200
and can be quite triggering

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.210
for a lot of people with
that generational trauma.

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:46.860
Is that really important to you
to do those kind of stories?

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:48.720
- I do think it's important,
but I think you also

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.220
have to do it in a way
that's compassionate

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:55.110
because trauma is a hard thing to look at.

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:59.220
And so I think that's
why in the world of film

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:01.260
and television and the art world,

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:05.287
we can find ways to make it accessible

00:09:06.268 --> 00:09:07.920
and to make it okay to look.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.380
- And a learning thing.
- And learn, yeah.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:11.730
Exactly.
- I agree.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:13.530
Well, what are the most important things

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:15.880
that you're trying to
teach your son right now?

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:20.520
- Well, my son is 11 now and you know,

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.340
he's really, it's kind of
the beginning of growing,

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:25.830
I can see him growing into a little man.

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:27.180
- [Kelly Clarkson] Oh, that freaks me out.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:29.416
- Right?
- My son is six. I'm sweating.

00:09:29.416 --> 00:09:30.660
- Oh, he's only six.
- Yeah.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:31.493
- Oh gosh.

00:09:31.493 --> 00:09:32.550
- I don't want him to grow up! (laughs)

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:34.044
- I know.

00:09:34.044 --> 00:09:37.170
It's really interesting because I just,

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:38.670
I respect him so much.

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.850
Like, I love the little
man that he's becoming

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:45.390
and his thoughtfulness
and sort of observation

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:47.940
of the world and I really want to like,

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:49.200
let that live for him.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.800
But, you know, I also
realize as he gets older

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:54.720
he's gonna be coming into a world that

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:57.120
has very strong expectations for him.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:59.340
I have very strong
expectations for him in terms

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:03.150
of respecting everybody around
him and just who he has to be

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:04.110
in the world.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:07.860
And you know, it's especially,

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:11.130
you know, my son is a young black boy

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:14.010
and as he gets older,
the culture is gonna look

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:15.720
at him differently.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:17.073
- Is that hard to teach?

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:20.683
- It's a real learning
curve for me to teach him.

00:10:20.683 --> 00:10:21.516
- But it's important.

00:10:21.516 --> 00:10:22.860
- I know it's my responsibility.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:24.360
So I start with,

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.180
I just wanna give him an
environment that has the most love

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:28.920
and the most security

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:30.603
because I know that that will fill him up.

00:10:30.603 --> 00:10:31.920
- That foundation.

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.020
- And give him a foundation so

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:36.120
that then hopefully it can
help protect him when he's

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:39.210
out in the world and give him
respect and accountability.

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:42.060
And you know, it's scary
though 'cause they're kids.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:43.050
He's an 11 year old kid.

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:45.960
He doesn't know anything and
all they do is make mistakes

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:47.670
and all they do is screw up.

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.310
And half the time they don't
even know what they've done.

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:52.140
And so it's, you know,

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:53.790
I'm starting to be like,

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:55.200
- It's hard, those big issues.

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:59.190
- You wanna try to anticipate
what they're gonna be facing

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:02.422
and that's just one of the
tricky parts of being a mom.

00:11:02.422 --> 00:11:03.270
- It's like anticipating, yeah.

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:05.670
It's the balance of
anticipating but also going,

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:09.000
I don't wanna rip that innocence from you

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:10.590
quite yet, you know?

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:11.937
It's hard.
- That's right.

00:11:11.937 --> 00:11:13.740
I want him to be able to make mistakes.

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:14.573
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:11:14.573 --> 00:11:16.380
- That's important. We have
to be able to make mistakes.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:18.390
It's part of our learning and growing.

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:20.130
So it's really that balance.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:21.810
Oh, being a mama, it's all that balance.

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:24.840
- Oh yeah. I talk about
it regularly in therapy.

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:27.120
So how do you help him
process what he sees going on

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:27.953
in the world?

00:11:27.953 --> 00:11:29.940
'Cause there are some big,
huge, major issues going on.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:33.840
I was asked, we just a while
back went to Uvalde, Texas,

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:34.710
my home state.

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:37.860
And just, I was asked by
one of the people involved

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:38.827
in the organization, like,

00:11:38.827 --> 00:11:40.830
"Have you talked to your kids about this?"

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:44.580
And I'm like, oh. I'm like,
that's a hard conversation.

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:46.410
Like how do you not terrify them,

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:48.210
but ready them and prepare them?

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:50.580
Like, it's a really
hard thing to navigate.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:51.930
- I know. It's so hard.
- It's a very difficult thing.

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:53.730
And you said having a young black boy

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:54.690
and you're a white mother,

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:56.850
that's a whole other thing of issues.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:59.790
So yeah. How do you do that?
How do you do those big-

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:00.623
- It's really-

00:12:00.623 --> 00:12:02.340
- Do you have a group of
people, like friends or?

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:05.220
- I do, but you know, I find
everybody does it differently.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:09.810
And you know, I'm a single
mom and he's an only child

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:13.680
and so we have like a really
special, close relationship.

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:17.580
And I try to let him,

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:20.970
I try to let him find the space to,

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:22.620
as these things are coming up

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:25.050
and we are having the conversation,

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:28.740
I try to like gauge where
he is with it, you know?

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:32.883
And honestly he's, now that he's 11,

00:12:34.407 --> 00:12:36.743
he's starting to have
awareness of these things.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:40.260
The other day, it was so cute, you know,

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:44.490
the queen had just passed
away and he, (chuckles)

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:45.960
we hadn't talked about it.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:48.847
And he came, we were getting
ready for school and he said,

00:12:48.847 --> 00:12:51.720
"Mom, did you hear about the queen?"

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:53.910
I was like, "Oh, I know, I know.

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:56.490
She was queen for so, so, so long."

00:12:56.490 --> 00:13:00.030
He goes, "It's just so sad." (laughs)

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:01.470
- Like to see your little man,

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:04.094
like have a conversation
like that is so crazy.

00:13:04.094 --> 00:13:04.927
- And it was so cute

00:13:04.927 --> 00:13:07.560
because I thought he has no connection

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:09.600
to the queen whatsoever.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:11.370
- But to show empathy.
- He had no idea that she

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:14.280
was queen for so many decades.

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:16.140
- But that little like sliver of empathy,

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:16.973
you know what I'm saying?

00:13:16.973 --> 00:13:17.940
That's nice.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:21.180
- And so I think getting back
to your point, it's like it is

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:24.960
about the empathy and it's
about gauging where they are

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:26.430
with their own empathy and

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:31.430
with their own ability to
handle the big topics, you know?

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:33.510
- I let mine come to me mostly.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:34.343
- Me too.

00:13:34.343 --> 00:13:35.176
- Yeah, 'cause I'm like, you know what,

00:13:35.176 --> 00:13:37.050
all kids are different, and
both my kids are so different.

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:39.690
Those conversations are far apart.

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:41.550
They're very different developmentally.

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:43.350
So I let them come to me

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:44.790
and I hope that's the right decision

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:45.810
but if not, well,

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:47.592
we all gotta be in
therapy for something, so.

00:13:47.592 --> 00:13:49.830
- (laughs) Well, we're screwing it up.

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:51.443
- [Kelly Clarkson] That's your thing.

